2. Technical Round 2 (22 candidates)

The second technical round began with the interviewer asking me to introduce myself.

1. I was asked to code the right view of a binary tree on their laptop.

2. The interviewer then asked me to solve the same problem without using a Queue, suggesting a DFS approach.

private void dfs(TreeNode node, int depth, List<Integer> result) {

if (node == null) return;

if (depth == result.size()) {

result.add(node.val);

} else {

result.set(depth, node.val);

}

dfs(node.left, depth + 1, result);

dfs(node.right, depth + 1, result);

}

3. I was asked to write a function to verify whether a given tree is a BST(binary search tree). All elements to the left must be smaller than the root and all elements to the right must be greater than the root.

private boolean validate(TreeNode node, long min, long max) {

if (node == null) return true;

if (node.val <= min || node.val >= max) return false;

return validate(node.left, min, node.val) && validate(node.right, node.val, max);

}

4. Next, I was given a SQL question involving two tables: employees and customers, each having id, name, salary, and department. Since departments could be common across both tables, the task was to find the employee name and salary, with the maximum salary from each department from both tables.

CREATE VIEW unified_people AS

SELECT id, name, salary, department FROM employees

UNION ALL

SELECT id, name, salary, department FROM customers;

SELECT name, salary, department

FROM unified_people up

WHERE salary = (

SELECT MAX(salary)

FROM unified_people

WHERE department = up.department

);

5. The discussion then moved to distributed computing, specifically the master-slave architecture, based on one of the projects mentioned on my resume.

6. I was also asked about my patents.

7. I raised a question about the absence of AI in the job description, considering I come from the CSE AIML branch.

Tip: Always remember to ask for the interviewer's name if they don't introduce themselves, as it might be asked in a subsequent round.

